//BUG="a global's initial value cannot mention another global"
//OUT="3"

// `normalize_ast` (src/compiler/normalizer/mod.rs) normalizes the initial
// value of every global before it enters any function. A name is looked up
// in `SymbolTable::variable_name_map`, and the globals of a file are only
// copied into that map by `push_function`, on the way into a function body.
// At the top level the map is still empty, so the `a` in `a + 2` is unknown
// and the compile fails with "Variable with name a not found." — about a
// variable declared on the line above.
//
// An initial value that names no other global works: a literal, `1 + 2`,
// `-1`, a string, a struct literal (02_variables/12 and 20). This case used
// to fail earlier than it does now — the lowerer skipped global initializers
// altogether, so any operator in one hit an `unreachable!()` in the
// normalizer. That half is fixed; the lookup is what is left.
//
// Inside a function the same expression is fine, because the function's
// scope starts out holding the globals. The initial values are executed at
// the top of `main` anyway, so the fix is to normalize them in a scope that
// has the globals declared so far.

a = 1
b = a + 2

fn main
    out "{b}"
